{"id": "concept-01", "type": "concept", "input": "In linear regression, what does the squared error loss measure, and why is there a 1/2 in front of it?", "expected_sections": ["3.1.1"], "key_facts": ["the squared error is the square of the difference between the prediction and the label", "the 1/2 makes no real difference but is notationally convenient: it cancels when taking the derivative"]}
{"id": "concept-02", "type": "concept", "input": "Does linear regression have a closed-form solution? When does it exist?", "expected_sections": ["3.1.1"], "key_facts": ["yes, an analytic solution w* = (X^T X)^{-1} X^T y", "it is unique when the design matrix X has full rank (no feature linearly dependent on the others)"]}
{"id": "concept-03", "type": "concept", "input": "Why is minimising squared error a sensible choice for linear regression from a probability point of view?", "expected_sections": ["3.1.3"], "key_facts": ["assuming additive Gaussian noise on the observations", "minimising squared error is equivalent to maximum likelihood estimation under that assumption"]}
{"id": "concept-04", "type": "concept", "input": "What does weight decay add to the loss function?", "expected_sections": ["3.7.1"], "key_facts": ["a penalty proportional to the squared L2 norm of the weights, (lambda/2)||w||^2", "lambda is a non-negative regularisation constant chosen on validation data", "larger lambda constrains the weights more"]}
{"id": "concept-05", "type": "concept", "input": "Why use an L2 penalty rather than an L1 penalty?", "expected_sections": ["3.7.1"], "key_facts": ["L2 biases the model towards distributing weight evenly across many features", "L1 tends to concentrate weight on a few features and clear others to zero (feature selection)"]}
{"id": "concept-06", "type": "concept", "input": "What does the softmax function do to the outputs of a classifier?", "expected_sections": ["4.1.1"], "key_facts": ["it exponentiates each logit and divides by the sum, so outputs are non-negative and sum to 1", "the largest logit still corresponds to the most likely class"]}
{"id": "concept-07", "type": "concept", "input": "What loss do we use for softmax regression, and what is its gradient with respect to the logits?", "expected_sections": ["4.1.2"], "key_facts": ["the cross-entropy loss", "the derivative with respect to logit o_j is softmax(o)_j - y_j, the predicted probability minus the one-hot label"]}
{"id": "concept-08", "type": "concept", "input": "What is wrong with purely linear models that makes us want hidden layers?", "expected_sections": ["5.1.1"], "key_facts": ["linearity implies monotonicity: increasing a feature always moves the output in the same direction", "many real problems violate this, so we add hidden layers to model more general functions"]}
{"id": "concept-09", "type": "concept", "input": "Why does a multilayer perceptron need a nonlinear activation function?", "expected_sections": ["5.1.1"], "key_facts": ["without a nonlinearity, the hidden layer can be collapsed and the network is equivalent to a single-layer linear (affine) model"]}
{"id": "concept-10", "type": "concept", "input": "What is ReLU, and why is it so popular?", "expected_sections": ["5.1.2"], "key_facts": ["ReLU(x) = max(x, 0)", "its derivatives are well behaved (either vanish or let the argument through), which makes optimisation easier and mitigates vanishing gradients"]}
{"id": "concept-11", "type": "concept", "input": "Why does training a neural network need more memory than just making predictions?", "expected_sections": ["5.3.4"], "key_facts": ["backpropagation reuses intermediate values computed in forward propagation", "those values must be kept until backpropagation is complete"]}
{"id": "concept-12", "type": "concept", "input": "What happens in gradient descent if the learning rate is too small or too large?", "expected_sections": ["12.3.1"], "key_facts": ["too small: x updates very slowly and needs many more iterations", "too large: the first-order approximation breaks down and the solution can oscillate or diverge"]}
{"id": "cross-01", "type": "cross", "input": "How does minibatch SGD relate to plain SGD, and why is it usually preferred?", "expected_sections": ["12.4", "12.5"], "key_facts": ["SGD uses one example per update, so each update costs O(1) instead of O(n)", "minibatch SGD processes batches, trading off SGD's statistical efficiency against the computational efficiency of large batches", "minibatch SGD is usually faster to converge in clock time"]}
{"id": "cross-02", "type": "cross", "input": "In what sense are linear regression and softmax regression both neural networks?", "expected_sections": ["3.1", "4.1"], "key_facts": ["linear regression can be seen as a single-layer fully connected network with one output", "softmax regression is also a single-layer fully connected network, with one output per class"]}
{"id": "cross-03", "type": "cross", "input": "How do backpropagation and gradient descent work together when training a network?", "expected_sections": ["5.3", "12.3"], "key_facts": ["backpropagation computes the gradients of the loss with respect to the parameters by traversing the computational graph in reverse", "gradient descent moves the parameters a step of size eta in the direction of the negative gradient"]}
{"id": "cross-04", "type": "cross", "input": "How does adding weight decay change the minibatch SGD update for linear regression?", "expected_sections": ["3.1", "3.7"], "key_facts": ["the plain update subtracts the learning rate times the average minibatch gradient", "with weight decay the weights are first multiplied by (1 - eta*lambda), shrinking them towards zero at each step"]}
